## ===========================================================================
## COMMERCE STREET — Book 3 (`story.ni:4197-4950`), Chapter 2's first half.
##
## The change document ("Chapter 2 — Commerce Street and Lord's Market",
## 2026-09-01) authorizes the source's first-visit layer as written: the
## street and its three shops, East Commerce Street's estates as locked
## fronts, no pressure with teeth, Jack back in the urchin's look. Every line
## below is Gentry's day text; the night variants belong to later chapters.
## Conversations are stubs until Phases 10/11 finish (standing rule) — the
## keepers arrive with the next increment, this file is the rooms.
##
## ARRIVAL — the change back (change document, "The clothes", the source's
## own automatic change, `story.ni:4266-4276`): on the first arrival the
## dress and the fashionable hat go into the satchel and the woolen cap comes
## back to Jack; the gray cloak is wherever the slide left it. GAP, reported
## not decided: Chord has no runtime statement that puts a garment ON — the
## cap can be moved to Jack but not worn for her — so the cap arrives in her
## hands and the source's own exit rule ("You'd better put your hat on") does
## the rest. The arrival text is David's (TODO); Gentry's block is kept in the
## comments below.
##
## Not carried, noted: `enter <storefront>` cannot redirect to the walk
## (Chord has no "try going"), so the storefronts describe and the exits
## walk; the bare `in` ("You'll have to say which shop") likewise; the shops'
## smell lines; the `[if it is day]` tails are carried as the day text.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE STREET
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Commerce Street
  a room
  aka commerce, the street, avenue
  west to the Eastern Junction
  east to the East Commerce Street
  northwest to the Bakery, one-way
  north to the Butcher's Shop, one-way
  northeast to the Armory, one-way
  west is blocked: commerce-lay-low
  east is blocked while the woolen cap is not worn: commerce-hat-on
  northwest is blocked while the woolen cap is not worn: commerce-hat-on
  north is blocked while the woolen cap is not worn: commerce-hat-on
  northeast is blocked while the woolen cap is not worn: commerce-hat-on
  first time
    (TODO during play-testing — "You made it." — Gentry's arrival block is
    kept aside in this file's comments; it ends by removing the cloak.)

  Commerce Street runs east to west, between Grubber's Market and Lord's
  Market. Raised sidewalks allow pedestrians to avoid the mud, horse dung, and
  occasional teamster's cart rumbling by. The shops here are actual buildings,
  spaced evenly along the avenue. Storefronts for a bakery, a butcher's shop,
  and an armory line the north side of the street from west to east.

  after the player entering while the player's market-escape has started
    reset the player's market-escape
  end after

  after the player entering while Jack is dressed
    move the dress to the cloth satchel
    move the fashionable hat to the cloth satchel
    move the woolen cap to the player
    change Jack to urchin
  end after

## Gentry's arrival block (`story.ni:4266`), verbatim, for David to rework:
##   "You made it.
##   You let out a long, shaky breath. You're safe now. Commerce Street is
##   home to a slightly more upscale class of merchant, and business here is
##   not quite as frantic as in Grubber's Market. Transactions tend to be
##   calmer and more deliberate, and involve significantly more money. The
##   Lord's Guard will be far less tolerant of a pack of armed bullies
##   menacing the citizens of the upper class, so it's unlikely that the
##   mercenaries will follow you here. And even if they did, there are too
##   many streets and side alleys for them to block off your escape.
##   Gratefully, you remove the cloak, which was beginning to stick to your
##   skin with all the sweat. Now it's time to pause, catch your breath, and
##   try to figure out why they were after you in the first place."

## `story.ni:4280` — by day only in the source; the port has no night yet, so
## the refusal stands until the chapter that walks back west at night.

define phrase commerce-lay-low
  It would be a good idea to lay low for a while before returning to Grubber's
  Market.
end phrase

## `story.ni:4284-4289`, the merchants' variant; the Bobby variant is
## Chapter 3's.

define phrase commerce-hat-on
  You'd better put your hat on — most of the merchants on this street know you
  as Jack. Keeping your hair stuffed up and hidden helps the disguise.
end phrase

create the sidewalk
  aka sidewalks, flagstone, flagstones, raised sidewalks
  scenery
  in the Commerce Street

  The sidewalks are made of flagstones laid over the cobbles of the street.

create the cobbles
  aka cobblestones, cobblestone, cobble, street stones, stone, stones
  scenery, plural
  in the Commerce Street

  The cobblestones are slick with mud.

create the mud
  aka dung, filth, horse dung
  scenery
  in the Commerce Street

  This sort of filth accumulates in most of the streets in Toresal, except for
  the richer districts.

## The storefronts (`story.ni:4231-4248`). Each describes; the walk is the
## exit it names.

create the bakery storefront
  aka bakery, baker, bakers, bakery sign, bakery door, loaf sign
  scenery
  in the Commerce Street

  A hanging sign above the door depicts a loaf of bread, and the warm smell of
  fried dough wafts out from the open door, making your stomach rumble.

create the butcher's storefront
  aka butchers storefront, butcher, butchers, butchery, pig, painted pig, pig sign
  scenery
  in the Commerce Street

  A brightly painted pig decorates the sign over this shop. The smell of burned
  sausage masks the darker odor of butchered animals.

create the armory storefront
  aka armory, armoury, armorers, armourers, armourer, armory sign, shield sign
  scenery
  in the Commerce Street

  The walls of this shop are built of heavy, blackened bricks — a grim exterior
  to house grim business. The sign over the door is painted with an axe and
  shield.

create the shopfronts
  aka shops, stores, storefronts, shop fronts, store fronts, signs, shop signs, doors, shop doors, buildings
  scenery, plural
  in the Commerce Street

  Storefronts for a bakery, a butcher's shop, and an armory line the north side
  of the street from west to east.

